<h1>Laws of Agents, Halachah 1</h1>
<p class="heb-title">הלכות שלוחין א</p>
<p class="note">Likutay Halachos, Choshen Mishpat. Based on Likutay Moharan I:4 ("Anochi Hashem Elokecha") and I:31, and I:65 ("Vayomer Boaz").<br>
Translated from the Hebrew.</p>

<p>A person's agent is like himself. There is no agency for a minor, etc.</p>

<p>For it is known that all Yisroel — their nefashos are one. For their root is from the aspect of "kulo echad, kulo tov" ["entirely one, entirely good"]. And as it is written (Sh'mos 1:5): "Kol hanefesh haba'ah livais Yaakov shiv'im nafesh" ["All the souls that came to the house of Yaakov were seventy souls"] — in the singular language [i.e., "nefesh" not "nefashos"], as Rashi explains. And therefore "sh'lucho shel adam kamoaso" ["a person's agent is like himself"] — for "kula chad" ["it is all one"], as above.</p>

<p>And therefore a nochri [gentile] cannot become an agent. For they are from behind the k'dushah, and are separated and divided from the k'dushah of Yisroel. And therefore one cannot say regarding them "sh'lucho kamoaso" — for this is the aspect of achdus [unity], as above. And therefore it applies only among Yisroel, who are all one and are all equal in the root of the achdus. But the akum are separated from the achdus, as above. And therefore an akum cannot become an agent — for it is impossible to say regarding them "sh'lucho kamoaso," as above.</p>

<p>And this is what Chazal said: "Just as you are b'nai b'ris [members of the covenant], so too your agents must be b'nai b'ris." For the sh'lichus [agency] is dependent on the b'ris. For there is the aspect of achdus. In the aspect of: "Uv'risi ne'emenes lo" ["And My covenant was faithful to him"] (T'hilim 89:29). Meaning, the aspect of emunah — which is the achdus, that one believes in His unity, Yisbarach — is dependent on the b'ris, as is brought in the words of Rabbainu, nairo ya'ir (Siman 31). And therefore the sh'lichus — which is only in the aspect of achdus, the aspect of "sh'lucho kamoaso," as above — therefore it applies only among b'nai b'ris, who are in the aspect of achdus, as above.</p>

<p>And therefore a katan [minor] cannot make an agent and cannot become an agent. For it is stated in the words of Rabbainu, nairo ya'ir, that the aspect of "kulo echad, kulo tov" is the completeness of da'as. In the aspect of: "Bayom hahu yih'yeh Hashem echad ush'mo echad" ["On that day Hashem will be one and His Name one"] (Z'charyah 14:9) — for then the da'as will be revealed. And it will be "kulo echad, kulo tov," as is brought at the beginning of the discourse "Anochi Hashem Elokecha" (Siman 4); see there. And therefore the katan, whose da'as is not yet complete — therefore he does not have the din of sh'lichus. For the main din of sh'lichus is through the aspect of achdus, which is dependent on da'as, as above.</p>

<p>And therefore ain shali'ach lidvar avairah [there is no agency for a transgression]. For through the avairah one is separated, chas v'shalom, from the root of the achdus. And therefore sh'lichus does not apply to an avairah. For, on the contrary, the avairah is separation and a blemish in the aspect of achdus, as is known. And the main sh'lichus is in the aspect of achdus, as above. And this is the aspect of "liskunai sh'dartich vila li'avusai" ["I sent you to rectify, not to corrupt"]. For the aspect of "echad" is "kulo tov," as above — and there is no corruption or ruination there, chas v'shalom. And therefore he can say to him "liskunai sh'dartich," etc. For certainly when I made you an agent — that you should be like me, which is the aspect of "kulo echad, kulo tov" — therefore certainly the sh'lichus was only for rectification and not for corruption. For the sh'lichus is only in the aspect of "echad" and "tov," as above.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of the chazan, who is called "sh'li'ach tzibbur" [agent of the congregation]. For the main tikkun of the tefillah is only that it should be in the aspect of "kulo echad, kulo tov," as is brought in the words of Rabbainu, nairo ya'ir, in the discourse "Vayomer Boaz," etc. (Siman 65). And therefore the main tikkun of the tefillah is through the sh'li'ach tzibbur. For the sh'li'ach tzibbur includes them all together and makes the tefillah one — as is explained above in Hilchos Sh'li'ach Tzibbur; see there. And this is the aspect of the chazaras hatefillah [the repetition of the tefillah]; see there. And therefore he is called "sh'li'ach tzibbur" — the aspect of "sh'lucho shel adam kamoaso," the aspect of "kulo echad," as above. And this is the tikkun of the tefillah, as above.</p>

<p>And this is what Chazal said: "'Gam' — 'gam' comes to include your agents. From here [we learn] that sh'lucho shel adam kamoaso." For "gam" is also the aspect of malchus — which is the aspect of emunah, the aspect of the daled of "Echad," as is brought in the writings of the Ari z"l — that the letters "gam" and "ais" are in malchus. And therefore the king holds them and gathers them together — so that they should not scatter, as Rashi explains on the verse "zeh ya'atzor bi'ami" ["this one shall rule over My people"] (Shmuel I 9:17); see there. For the malchus is the aspect of achdus, as above. And there are the roots of the nefashos of Yisroel — whose root is in achdus, as above. And therefore "gam" includes the agent — that sh'lucho shel adam kamoaso. For this is dependent on achdus, which is the aspect of malchus — the aspect of "gam," as above. And see in the Writings [of the Ari z"l] that "gam" is in Leah — which is the aspect of the daled of "Echad," the aspect of the kesher shel tefillin [the knot of the tefillin]; see there.</p>

<p>And this is: "Just as you are b'nai b'ris, so too your agents must be b'nai b'ris." For the tikkun of the malchus — the aspect of emunah, the aspect of achdus — all of this is dependent on the b'ris. For "the one who guards the b'ris merits malchus." And as it is written: "Uv'risi ne'emenes lo" — as above. And therefore sh'lichus applies only among b'nai b'ris, who are in the aspect of "kulo echad, kulo tov," as above.</p>


